the babylonian king Hammurabi was  responsible for establishing the  earliest known law code now probably  other rulers and States must have had  sets of laws but it's Hammurabi's code  of laws that's the earliest coherent  system that survives so he gets the  credit for it law codes are absolutely  vital if one is to have large cities  when you're in a little cell a  settlement or a village it's possible in  fact is is usually the common practice  for interpersonal disputes to be handled  by family elders and that kind of system  usually works because in these small  environments everybody knows everybody  else or at least is bound by some sort  of extended kinship tie but once you  start to have cities real urban centers  with big populations you end up with a  large number of strangers living and  interacting with one another in those  circumstances conflicts among  individuals inevitably arise and you  need to have some way that you can  mediate those in a relatively impartial  manner otherwise you would just have  chaos and you have people killing one  another and that is the great  contribution of law codes it provides a  set of rules to impersonally adjudicate  these disputes and to resolve them and  by doing so it makes big cities possible  now if you look at Hammurabi's law code  from a modern perspective admittedly  aspects of it might seem a bit harsh and  really unfair for example the prescribed  punishment for almost any offense is  death and all human beings in  Hammurabi's law code are most certainly  not treated equally so there are  different rules and punishments for  groups such as women the poor and slaves  in general Hammurabi's law code falls  into a category of law codes which are  termed retaliatory and what this means  is that what you do to someone else  typically gets done to you in return  many of the rules of the Old Testament  which was also formulated in the ancient  Near East during a second millennium BC  are of a similar retaliatory nature and  this is exemplified by the familiar  phrase an eye for an eye a tooth for a  tooth  that's a retaliatory law code at times  this reciprocal approach of Hammurabi's  code leads to some very creative  punishments so for example when a person  is discovered stealing from a burning  house the punishment is he himself is  condemned to be thrown into a fire or  when a doctor is botched in operation he  is then sentenced to have his hands  chopped off at other times the code  seems almost surprisingly sympathetic to  groups who often are marginalized in  these societies for example there's a  law in Hammurabi's code that prescribes  that if a man divorces his wife and  she's borne him a child  he must not only return her dowry but  also has to provide additional financial  support for raising the child and  whatever it's quirks  Hammurabi's code really represents a  huge step forward for all of  civilization because it makes it  possible for large numbers of people to  live in cities  you
 

